# Python's own leavings, and the artefacts a local run drops in the tree. The
# databases and the parquet belong in /data inside the container; they land here
# only when something is run against the working tree by hand.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.parquet

# Fitted models. Same story: they live in /data, but a local
# `python -m occupancy_forecast.train` run will drop them here.
*.pkl
models/

# The panel's toolchain. node_modules/ is 69 MB of it and has no business here.
#
# `*/panel/dist/` used to sit on the line below, and does not any more. The
# built bundle IS committed, and deliberately: the Dockerfile only COPYs
# `panel/dist`, nothing compiles a frontend on the Home Assistant box, and an
# add-on installed from this repository's URL gets nothing but a git clone --
# so an ignored dist/ is an add-on that cannot be installed from the store at
# all. It fails at the COPY, with a Docker error that says nothing about the
# panel. The cost is a minified bundle in every diff that touches the panel,
# which is real and is the smaller of the two.
#
# `scripts/build-panel.sh` writes `panel/dist/.source-hash` beside the bundle
# and the pre-commit hook checks it, so a source edit committed without a
# rebuild is refused rather than silently shipped.
node_modules/

# Contributor and agent instructions, kept local. Case matters on Linux, so this
# has to be the real spelling -- `claude.md` matches nothing.
CLAUDE.md

# NOT ignored, and deliberately: the STABLE add-on (occupancy-forecast/) is
# generated from occupancy-forecast-edge/ by scripts/promote.sh, but it is
# tracked in full, because it is what the HA box builds and git has to be able
# to say what stable is running. Its config.yaml, DOCS.md and CHANGELOG.md are
# hand-written and the rest is a copy of ../occupancy-forecast-edge. Edit the
# originals there, never the copies here.
